WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.560
We got this great question asking about the difference between Numpy and Polars.

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:11.920
At the high level, Numpy is meant for matrices, and matrices require homogeneous data types,

00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:16.240
so everything in the grid must be the exact same type, so for example,

00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:21.920
floating point numbers. This makes Numpy incredibly fast for things like linear algebra

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:27.920
and multidimensional math. Polars, on the other hand, is a data frame library. Data frames are

00:00:27.920 --> 00:00:34.560
built for tabular data, so think of like a spreadsheet, and with data frames, your columns

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:40.799
can be of different types, so you can have strings, integers, dates, and so on in the same table.

00:00:41.599 --> 00:00:48.160
In terms of their relationship, they both use structured data. Polars uses Apache Arrow and

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:54.240
is built on Rust, and it also does something called lazy execution, so it makes it incredibly fast

00:00:54.240 --> 00:01:01.119
for querying massive tables. And so, in conclusion, Numpy for complex matrix math,

00:01:01.119 --> 00:01:04.160
and Polars for fast tabular data processing.

